00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.950
on YouTube so that the
world can learn how to build

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.630
language models from scratch.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:02.600
OK.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:09.490
So why do we decide to make this
course and endure all the pain?

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.870
So let's ask GPT-4.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.590
So if you ask it, why teach
a course on building language

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:19.690
models from scratch, the
reply is, teaching a course

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.610
provides foundational
understanding of techniques,

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.210
fosters innovation.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.680
The typical generic blathers.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.380
OK, so here's the real reason.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:33.470
So we're in a bit of
a crisis, I would say.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.610
Researchers are becoming
more and more disconnected

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.800
from the underlying technology.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.850
Eight years ago, researchers
would implement and train

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:44.690
their own models in AI.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:49.390
Even six years ago, you would at
least take the models like BERT

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.710
and download them
and fine-tune them.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.910
And now, many people
can just get away

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:57.800
with prompting a
proprietary model.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.500
So this is not
necessarily bad, right,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.870
because as you enter these
layers of abstraction,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.130
we can all do more.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.220
And a lot of research
has been unlocked

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.460
by the simplicity of being able
to prompt a language model.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.490
And I do a fair
share of prompting.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.680
So there's nothing
wrong with that.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:21.020
But let's also remember that
these abstractions are leaky.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.470
So in contrast to programming
languages on operating systems,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.480
you don't really understand
what the abstraction is.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.100
It's a string in and
string out, I guess.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.720
And I would say
that there's still

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.530
a lot of fundamental research to
be done that requires tearing up

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:39.110
the stack and co-designing
different aspects of the data

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.400
and the systems and the model.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.870
And I think really that full
understanding of this technology

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.960
is necessary for
fundamental research.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.520
So that's why this class exists.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.610
We want to enable the
fundamental research

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:53.670
to continue.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.070
And our philosophy
is to understand it,

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.100
you have to build it.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.740
So there's one
small problem here.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.940
And this is because of
the industrialization

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.420
of language models.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:13.360
So GPT-4 has rumored to be
1.8 trillion parameters,

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.589
cost $100 million to train.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:22.120
You have xAI building the
clusters with 200,000 H100s,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.790
if you can imagine that.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:27.540
There's an investment
of over $500 billion,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.400
supposedly, over four years.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.570
So these are pretty
large numbers, right.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.300
And furthermore, there's no
public details on how these

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.820
models are being built.
Here from GPT-4--

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.940
this is even two years ago--

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.690
they very honestly say that due
to the competitive landscape

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:50.260
and safety limitations, we're
going to disclose no details.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:55.010
OK, so this is the state
of the world right now.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:59.060
And so in some sense, frontier
models are out of reach for us.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:00.790
So if you came into
this class thinking

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:06.670
you're each going to train
your own GPT-4, sorry.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.920
So we're going to build
small language models.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.180
But the problem is that these
might not be representative.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.290
And here are some of two
examples to illustrate why.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.700
So here's a simple one.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.500
If you look at the fraction of
FLOPs spent in the attention

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.500
layers of a transformer
versus an MLP,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.070
this changes quite a bit.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.760
So this is a tweet
from Stephen Roller

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.720
from quite a few years ago.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:39.130
But this is still true.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:40.780
If you look at small
models, it looks

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.560
like the number of FLOPs in the
attention versus the MLP layers

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.060
are roughly comparable.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:52.710
But if you go up to 175 billion,
then the MLPs really dominate.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.020
Right, so why does this matter?

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.980
Well, if you spend a lot
of time at small scale

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.150
and you're optimizing
the tension,

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.240
you might be optimizing
the wrong thing,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:08.930
because at larger scale,
it gets washed out.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.870
This is a simple example,
because you can literally

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:15.080
make this plot without
actually any compute.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.900
It's napkin math.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.720
Here's something that's a little
bit harder to grapple with,

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.170
is this emergent behavior.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:25.920
So this is a paper from
Jason Wei from 2022.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.950
And here, this plot shows
that as you increase

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.950
the amount of
training FLOPs and you

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.510
look at accuracy on
a bunch of tasks,

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:40.080
you'll see that for a while,
it looks like the accuracy,

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.480
nothing is happening.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:45.170
And all of a sudden,
you get these emergent

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:48.150
of various phenomena,
like in-context learning.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.630
So if you were hanging
around at this scale,

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.300
you would be
concluding that well,

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.710
these language models
really don't work.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:58.990
When, in fact, you had to
scale up to get that behavior.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:02.120
So don't despair.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:05.260
We can still learn
something in this class.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:08.690
But we have to be very precise
about what we're learning.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.550
So there's three
types of knowledge.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.880
There's the mechanics
of how things work.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:15.230
This, we can teach you.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:16.970
We can teach you what
a transformer is.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:18.590
You can implement a transformer.

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:21.130
We can teach you how model
parallelism leverages

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:22.490
GPUs efficiently.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:27.260
These are just the raw
ingredients, the mechanics.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:28.900
So that's fine.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.880
We can also teach you mindset.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.700
So this is something
a bit more subtle

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.890
and seems a little bit fuzzy.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.750
But this is actually,
in some ways,

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:42.290
more important, I would say.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:46.120
Because the mindset
that we're going to take

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.010
is that we want
to squeeze as most

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:51.456
out of the hardware as possible
and take scaling seriously.

00:07:51.456 --> 00:07:54.590
Right, because in some sense,
the mechanics, all of those

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:56.510
will see later that all
of these ingredients

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:58.440
have been around for a while.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.040
But it was really, I
think, the scaling mindset

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:06.170
that OpenAI pioneered that led
to this next generation of AI

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.340
models.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:08.930
So mindset, I
think, hopefully we

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:12.900
can bang into you that to
think in a certain way.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.740
And then thirdly is intuitions.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:19.640
And this is about
which data and modeling

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.780
decisions lead to good models.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:25.500
This, unfortunately, we can
only partially teach you.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.070
And this is because
what architectures

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:31.310
and what data sets
work at low scales

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.480
might not be the same ones
that work at large scales.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.020
But hopefully you got
2 and 1/2 out of three.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.669
So that's pretty good
bang for your buck.

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:47.710
OK, speaking of intuitions,
there's a sort of, I guess,

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:51.690
sad reality of things that
you can tell a lot of stories

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.630
about why certain things
in the transformer

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:55.600
are the way they are.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.670
But sometimes it's just
you do the experiments

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.890
and the experiments speak.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.963
So, for example, there's
this known Shazeer paper

00:09:03.963 --> 00:09:05.880
that introduced the
SwiGLU, which is something

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.210
that we'll see a bit
more in this class, which

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:11.910
is a type of non-linearity.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:16.420
And in the conclusion, the
results are quite good.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:17.592
And this got adopted.

00:09:17.592 --> 00:09:19.800
But in the conclusion, there
is this honest statement

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.560
that we offer no
explanation except for,

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.640
this is divine benevolence.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.530
So there you go.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:32.310
This is the extent
of our understanding.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.680
OK, so now let's talk
about this bitter lesson

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:37.780
that I'm sure people
have heard about.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:41.460
I think there's a misconception
that a bitter lesson means

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:45.130
that scale is all that matters,
algorithms don't matter.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:48.370
All you do is pump more
capital into building the model

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:49.730
and you're good to go.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:52.280
I think this couldn't be
further from the truth.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:55.570
I think the right interpretation
is that algorithms at scale

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:57.230
is what matters.

00:09:57.230 --> 00:10:01.150
And because at the end of the
day, your accuracy of your model

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:03.640
is really a product
of your efficiency

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:07.090
and the number of
resources you put in.

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:10.940
And actually, efficiency,
if you think about it,

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:13.250
is way more important
at larger scale.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:16.400
Because if you're spending
hundreds of millions of dollars,

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.780
you cannot afford to be
wasteful in the same way that

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:25.790
if you're looking at running
a job on your local cluster,

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:26.930
you might run it again.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:28.730
You fail, you debug it.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:33.200
And if you look at actually
the utilization and the use,

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:37.150
I'm sure OpenAI is way more
efficient than any of us

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:38.000
right now.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.380
So efficiency
really is important.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.540
And furthermore, I think
this point is maybe

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:50.270
not as well appreciated in the
scaling rhetoric, so to speak.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:52.770
Which is that if you
look at efficiency,

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.080
which is a combination of
hardware and algorithms,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.450
but if you just look at
the algorithm efficiency,

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:02.720
there's this nice open ed
paper from 2020 that showed,

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:09.740
over the period of 2012 to 2019,
there was a 44x algorithmic

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:13.850
efficiency improvement in the
time that it took to train

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:16.316
ImageNet to a certain
level of accuracy.

00:11:16.316 --> 00:11:18.360
Right, so this is huge.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:19.730
And I think if you--

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:22.460
I don't know if you could
see the abstract here.

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:24.460
This is faster than Moore's law.

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:26.730
Right, so algorithms do matter.

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:29.690
If you didn't have
this efficiency,

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:33.030
you would be paying
44 times more cost.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.840
This is for image models,
but there's some results

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.030
for language as well.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:42.020
OK, so with all that, I think
the right framing or mindset

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:44.250
to have is, what is
the best model one

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:47.880
can build given a certain
compute and data budget?

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:48.430
OK.

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:50.220
And this question
makes sense no matter

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:56.670
what scale you're at, because
it's accuracy per resources.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.890
And of course, if you
can raise the capital

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:01.210
and get more resources,
you'll get better models.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:04.740
But as researchers, our goal
is to improve the efficiency

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:07.170
of the algorithms.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.550
OK, so maximize efficiency.

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:12.390
We're going to
hear a lot of that.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:18.630
OK, so now let me talk a little
bit about the current landscape.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:23.520
And a little bit of, I
guess, obligatory history.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.750
So language models have
been around for a while now.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:31.710
Going back to Shannon, who
looked at language models

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:35.730
as a way to estimate
the entropy of English.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:40.380
I think in AI, they really
were prominent in NLP,

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:42.370
where they were a
component of larger

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:45.020
systems like machine translation
and speech recognition.

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:48.460
And one thing that's maybe
not as appreciated these days

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:51.250
is that if you
look back in 2007,

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:55.430
Google was training fairly
large N-gram models.

00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:57.560
So 5-gram models, over
two trillion tokens,

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:00.520
which is a lot more
tokens than GPT-3.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:03.850
And it was only, I guess,
in the last two years

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:08.230
that we've gotten to
that in token count.

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:10.900
But they were N-gram models,
so they didn't really

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:12.580
exhibit any of the
interesting phenomena

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.220
that we know of
language models today.

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:18.930
OK, so in the 2010s, I think a
lot of the-- you can think about

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:19.430
this.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:21.490
A lot of the deep
learning revolution

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:25.940
happened and a lot of the
ingredients falling into place.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:27.820
Right, so there was the
first neural language

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:32.180
model from Joshua Bengio's
group and back in 2003.

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:34.365
There was seq-to-seq models.

00:13:34.365 --> 00:13:37.900
This, I think, was
a big deal for,

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:41.180
how do you basically
model sequences?

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:45.530
From Ilya and Google folks.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:48.200
There's an Adam
optimizer, which still

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:52.580
is used by the majority of
people dating over a decade ago.

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.500
There's a tension
mechanism, which

00:13:54.500 --> 00:14:00.260
was developed in the context of
machine translation, which then

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:03.570
led up to the famous
attention all you need,

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:07.140
or AKA, the transformer
paper in 2017.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:10.230
People were looking at how
to scale mixture of experts.

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:16.460
There was a lot of work around
late 2010s on how to essentially

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.180
do model parallelism.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.940
And they were
actually figuring out

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:23.130
how you could train 100
billion parameter models.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:25.640
They didn't train it for
very long, because these

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.420
were more systems work.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:34.070
But all the ingredients were
in place before by the time

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:37.710
the 2020 came around.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:43.620
So I think one other trend,
which was starting NLP,

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.320
was the idea of these
foundation models

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.450
that could be trained
on a lot of text

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:51.550
and adapted to a wide
range of downstream tasks.

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:55.360
So ELMo, BERT, T5.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:15:01.750
These were models that were,
for their time, very exciting.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:04.170
We maybe forget
how excited people

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:05.770
were about things like BERT.

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:09.300
But it was a big deal.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:11.680
I mean, this is
abbreviated history,

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:18.870
but I think one critical
piece of the puzzle is OpenAI.

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:24.120
Taking these ingredients and
applying very nice engineering

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:28.930
and really pushing
on the scaling laws.

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:32.650
Embracing it as this
is the mindset piece.

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:35.490
And that led to GPT-2 and GPT-3.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:43.420
Google obviously was in the game
and trying to compete as well.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:48.760
But that paved the way,
I think, to another line

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:52.700
of work, which is these
were all closed models.

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:55.970
So models that weren't released
and you can only access via API.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:59.200
But there were also
open models, starting

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:03.980
with the early work by Eleuther
right after GPT-3 came out.

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:08.620
Meta's early attempt, which
didn't work maybe as quite as

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:09.670
well.

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:10.900
BLOOM.

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:14.710
And then Meta, Alibaba,
DeepSeek, AI2, and there's

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:17.050
a few others which
I haven't listed,

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:22.120
have been creating
these open models where

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.640
the weights are released.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:28.220
One other piece of, I think,
tidbit about openness,

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:31.160
I think is important, is that
there's many levels of openness.

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:33.380
There's closed
models, like GPT-4.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:37.460
There's open weight models,
where the weights are available.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:39.680
And there's actually a
paper, a very nice paper

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:41.370
with lots of
architectural details,

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:44.550
but no details
about the data set.

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:48.890
And then there's open source
models, where all the weights

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:50.040
and data are available.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.070
And the paper where
they're honestly trying

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:56.030
to explain as much as they can.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:17:00.090
But of course, you can't really
capture everything in a paper,

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:03.560
and there's no substitute
for learning how to build it

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:06.380
except for doing it yourself.

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:08.940
OK, so that leads
to the present day,

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:15.480
where there's a whole host of
frontier models from OpenAI,

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:19.410
Anthropic, xAI, Google, Meta,
DeepSeek, Alibaba, Tencent,

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:25.790
and probably a few others that
dominate the current landscape.

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:31.550
So we're in an interesting
time, where just to reflect,

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.410
a lot of the ingredients,
like I said, were developed.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:35.910
Which is good,
because I think we're

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:39.330
going to revisit some
of those ingredients

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:42.670
and trace how these
techniques work.

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:46.080
And then we're going to try
to move as close as we can

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.840
to best practices
on frontier models,

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:56.940
but using information from
essentially the open community.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:00.300
And reading between the lines
from what we know about the

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.910
closed models.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:07.470
OK, so just as an interlude.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:10.150
So what are you looking at here?

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:14.890
So this is an
executable lecture.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:17.580
So it's a program where
I'm stepping through,

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.450
and it delivers the
content of lecture.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.260
So one thing that I
think is interesting here

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:25.000
is that you can embed code.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:28.930
So you can just
step through code.

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:31.450
And I think this is a smaller
screen than I'm used to,

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:34.150
but you can look at the
environment variables

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:35.780
as you're stepping through code.

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:38.530
So that's useful
later when we start

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:42.075
actually trying to drill down
and giving code examples.

00:18:42.075 --> 00:18:44.200
You can see the hierarchical
structure of a lecture

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:46.090
like we're in this
module and you can see

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:48.520
where it was called from main.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:52.970
And you can jump to definitions,
like supervised fine tuning,

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.930
which we'll talk about later.

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:56.510
OK.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:19:00.490
And if you think this looks
