So here, you implement a
tokenizer, model architecture,

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:54.740
and training.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:57.810
So I'll just say a bit more
about what these components are.

00:27:57.810 --> 00:28:02.930
So a tokenizer is something
that converts between strings

00:28:02.930 --> 00:28:05.490
and sequences of integers.

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:08.480
Intuitively, you can think
about the integers corresponding

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:12.980
to breaking up the
string into segments

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:15.170
and mapping each
segment to an integer.

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:18.780
And the idea is that
your sequence of integers

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:20.820
is what goes into the
actual model, which

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.970
has to be a fixed dimension.

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:26.190
OK, so in this
course, we'll talk

00:28:26.190 --> 00:28:32.520
about the byte pair encoding BPE
tokenizer, which is relatively

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:38.430
simple and still is used.

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:43.710
There are a promising
set of methods

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:45.310
on tokenizer-free approaches.

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:49.200
So these are methods that
just start with the raw bytes

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:52.800
and don't do
tokenization and develop

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:56.970
a particular architecture
that just takes the raw bytes.

00:28:56.970 --> 00:28:58.570
This work is promising.

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:02.980
But so far, I haven't seen it
been scaled to the frontier yet.

00:29:02.980 --> 00:29:05.940
So we'll go with BP for now.

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:06.490
OK.

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:09.900
So once you've tokenized
your sequence or strings

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:14.340
into a sequence of integers, now
we define a model architecture

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:16.340
over these sequences.

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:20.920
So the starting point here
is original transformer.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:28.060
That's what is the backbone of
basically all frontier models.

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:30.940
And here's our
architectural diagram.

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:36.110
We won't go into details here,
but there's a tension piece

00:29:36.110 --> 00:29:42.910
and then there's a MLP layer
with some normalization.

00:29:42.910 --> 00:29:48.020
So a lot has actually
happened since 2017, right.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:51.460
And I think there's a sense to
which all the transformer was

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:54.010
invented and then everyone's
just using transformer.

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:55.910
And to a first
approximation, that's true.

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:57.590
We're still using
the same recipe.

00:29:57.590 --> 00:30:02.440
But there have been a bunch
of smaller improvements that

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:05.450
do make a substantial difference
when you add them all up.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:11.120
So for example, there is the
non-linear activation function.

00:30:11.120 --> 00:30:13.580
So SwiGLU, which we saw
a little bit before.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:14.940
Positional embeddings.

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:17.570
There's new
positional embeddings.

00:30:17.570 --> 00:30:20.420
These rotary positional
embeddings, which we'll

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:22.400
talk about.

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:24.860
Normalization.

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:26.628
Instead of using
layer norm, we're

00:30:26.628 --> 00:30:28.670
going to look at something
called RMS norm, which

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:30.830
is similar but simpler.

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:33.500
There's a question of where you
place the normalization which

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:36.530
has been changed from
the original transformer.

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:41.070
The MLP use the canonical
version is a dense MLP,

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:44.240
and you can replace that
with mixture of experts.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:47.690
Attention is something that
has actually been gaining

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:50.570
a lot of attention, I guess.

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:53.870
There's full attention,
and then there's

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:56.100
sliding window attention
and linear attention.

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:59.790
All of these are trying to
prevent the quadratic blowup.

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:04.710
There's also lower dimensional
versions, like GQA and MLA,

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:07.220
which we'll get to in a second.

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:10.320
Or not in a second, but
in a future lecture.

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:13.620
And then the most
maybe radical thing

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:17.080
is other alternatives
to the transformer,

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:18.880
like state space
models like Hyena,

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:22.540
where they're not
doing attention,

00:31:22.540 --> 00:31:25.900
but some other operation.

00:31:25.900 --> 00:31:29.370
And sometimes you get
best of both worlds

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:32.670
by mixing, making a hybrid
model that mixes these

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:35.502
in with transformers.

00:31:35.502 --> 00:31:39.160
OK, so once you define your
architecture, you need to train.

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:42.880
So design decisions
include optimizer.

00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:48.420
So AdamW, which is a variant
basically Adam fixed up,

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:51.070
is still very prominent.

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:52.570
So we'll mostly work with that.

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:54.690
But it is worth
mentioning that there

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:57.390
is more recent optimizers
like Muon and SOAP

00:31:57.390 --> 00:32:00.330
that have shown promise.

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:02.490
Learning rate schedule.

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.230
Batch size.

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:06.400
Whether you do
regularization or not.

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:07.380
Hyperparameters.

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:10.300
There's a lot of details here.

00:32:10.300 --> 00:32:15.260
And I think this class is one
where the details do matter,

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:17.770
because you can easily
have order of magnitude

00:32:17.770 --> 00:32:20.890
difference between a
well tuned architecture

00:32:20.890 --> 00:32:24.310
and something that's just
a vanilla transformer.

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:27.070
So in Assignment
1, basically you'll

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:30.220
implement the BPE tokenizer.

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:33.910
I'll warn you that
this is actually

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:38.870
the part that seems to have
been a lot of surprising,

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:40.610
maybe a lot of work for people.

00:32:40.610 --> 00:32:43.940
So just, you're warned.

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:48.640
And you also implement the
transformer cross-entropy loss,

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.750
the AdamW optimizer
and training loop.

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:52.460
So again, the whole stack.

00:32:52.460 --> 00:32:57.800
And we're not making you
implement PyTorch from scratch.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:32:59.470
So you can use PyTorch.

00:32:59.470 --> 00:33:03.400
But you can't use the
transformer implementation

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:04.840
for PyTorch.

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:08.740
There's a small list of
functions that you can use,

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:10.870
and you can only use those.

00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:15.040
OK, so we're going to
have some tiny stories

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:17.570
and open web text data
sets that you'll train on.

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:19.780
And then there will
be a leaderboard

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:22.570
to minimize the OpenWeb
text perplexity.

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:26.660
We'll give you 90 minutes on an
H100 and see what you can do.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:30.190
So this is last year.

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:31.850
So see, we have the top.

00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:34.690
So this is the number
to beat for this year.

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:37.000
OK.

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:38.060
All right.

00:33:38.060 --> 00:33:39.610
So that's the basics.

00:33:39.610 --> 00:33:45.170
Now after basics, I mean, in
some sense, you're done, right.

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:48.380
You have ability to
train a transformer.

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:50.170
What else do you need?

00:33:50.170 --> 00:33:55.450
So the system part
really goes into how

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:57.418
you can optimize this further.

00:33:57.418 --> 00:33:59.210
So how do you get the
most out of hardware?

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:02.860
And for this, we need to take
a closer look at the hardware

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:05.450
and how we can leverage it.

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:08.050
So there's kernels,
parallelism, and inference

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:10.909
are the three
components of this unit.

00:34:10.909 --> 00:34:15.280
So OK, so to first
talk about kernels.

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:18.639
Let's talk a little bit
about what a GPU looks like.

00:34:18.639 --> 00:34:23.469
OK, so a GPU, which
we'll get much more into,

00:34:23.469 --> 00:34:30.850
is basically a huge array
of these little units that

00:34:30.850 --> 00:34:33.940
do floating point operations.

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:38.900
And maybe the one thing to note
is that this is the GPU chip,

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:44.889
and here is the memory
that's actually off chip.

00:34:44.889 --> 00:34:48.489
And then there's some other
memory, like L2 caches

00:34:48.489 --> 00:34:50.270
and L1 caches on chip.

00:34:50.270 --> 00:34:55.790
And so the basic idea is that
compute has to happen here.

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:57.800
Your data might
be somewhere else.

00:34:57.800 --> 00:35:00.550
And how do you basically
organize your compute

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:04.640
so that you can
be most efficient?

00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:10.220
So one quick analogy is
imagine that your memory

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:15.590
is where you can store your
data and the model parameters is

00:35:15.590 --> 00:35:16.790
like a warehouse.

00:35:16.790 --> 00:35:20.310
And your compute is
like the factory.

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:24.680
And what ends up
being a big bottleneck

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:28.490
is just data movement
costs, all right.

00:35:28.490 --> 00:35:32.280
So the thing that
we have to do is,

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:36.060
how do you organize the compute,
even a matrix multiplication,

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:39.830
to maximize the
utilization of the GPUs

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:42.300
by minimizing the data movement?

00:35:42.300 --> 00:35:43.730
And there's a
bunch of techniques

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:48.630
like fusion and tiling
that allow you to do that.

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:50.670
So we'll get all into
the details of that.

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:54.770
And to implement and
leverage the kernel,

00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:56.580
we're going to look at Triton.

00:35:56.580 --> 00:35:58.820
There's other things you
can do with various levels

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:00.950
of sophistication,
but we're going

00:36:00.950 --> 00:36:02.570
to use Triton,
which is developed

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:05.622
by OpenAI in a popular
way to build kernels.

00:36:05.622 --> 00:36:07.330
OK, so we're going to
write some kernels.

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:08.830
That's for one GPU.

00:36:08.830 --> 00:36:16.200
So now, in general, you have
these big runs take 10,000s,

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:18.610
if not tens of
thousands of GPUs.

00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:22.020
But even at eight, it
starts becoming interesting,

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:26.610
because you have a lot of GPUs
that are connected to some CPU

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:27.190
nodes.

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:32.850
And they also are directly
connected via NVSwitch, NVLink.

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:38.608
And it's the same idea, right.

00:36:38.608 --> 00:36:40.650
The only thing is that
data movement between GPUs

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:42.471
is even slower.

00:36:42.471 --> 00:36:45.390
Right, and so we
need to figure out

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:51.510
how to put model parameters
and activations and gradients

00:36:51.510 --> 00:36:54.780
and put them on the GPUs
and do the computation

00:36:54.780 --> 00:36:58.710
and to minimize the
amount of movement.

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:00.450
And then so we're
going to explore

00:37:00.450 --> 00:37:04.630
different type of techniques,
like data parallelism and tensor

00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:06.590
parallelism, and so on.

00:37:06.590 --> 00:37:11.080
So that's all I'll
say about that.

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:14.410
And finally,
inference is something

00:37:14.410 --> 00:37:18.940
that we didn't actually
do last year in the class.

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:20.710
Although we had a guest lecture.

00:37:20.710 --> 00:37:26.410
But this is important, because
inference is how you actually

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:27.350
use a model.

00:37:27.350 --> 00:37:30.280
Right, it's basically the
task of generating tokens

00:37:30.280 --> 00:37:32.870
given a prompt, given
a trained model.

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:35.410
And it also turns
out to be really

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:37.690
useful for a bunch
of other things

00:37:37.690 --> 00:37:42.320
besides just chatting
with your favorite model.

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:45.140
You need it for reinforcement
learning, test time compute,

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:48.050
which has been very
popular lately.

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:51.590
And even evaluating models,
you need to do inference.

00:37:51.590 --> 00:37:54.940
So we're going to spend some
time talking about inference.

00:37:54.940 --> 00:37:57.590
Actually, if you
think about globally,

00:37:57.590 --> 00:38:02.640
the cost that's
spent on inference,

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:07.730
it's eclipsing the cost that
it's used to train models.

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:10.950
Because training, despite
it being very intensive,

00:38:10.950 --> 00:38:12.630
is ultimately a one-time cost.

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:16.740
And inference is-- cost
scales with every use.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:19.830
And the more people
use your model,

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:23.300
the more you'll need
inference to be efficient.

00:38:23.300 --> 00:38:29.340
OK, so in inference,
there's two phases.

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:30.740
There's a pre-fill and a decode.

00:38:30.740 --> 00:38:33.140
Pre-fill is you take
the prompt and you

00:38:33.140 --> 00:38:36.780
can run it through the model
and get some activations.

00:38:36.780 --> 00:38:40.190
And then decode is you go
autoregressively one by one

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:41.990
and generate tokens.

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:45.740
So pre-fill, all the
tokens are given, so you

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:47.220
can process everything at once.

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:50.310
So this is exactly what
you see at training time.

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:52.430
And generally, this
is a good setting

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:55.070
to be in, because
you can parallel--

00:38:55.070 --> 00:38:58.230
it's naturally parallel and
you're mostly compute bound.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:38:59.970
What makes inference,
I think, it's

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:03.460
special and difficult is that
this autoregressive decoding,

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:05.320
you need to generate
one token at a time.

00:39:05.320 --> 00:39:08.070
And it's hard to actually
saturate all your GPUs,

00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:10.920
and it becomes memory bound
because you're constantly

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:12.640
moving data around.

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:16.920
And we'll talk about a few
ways to speed the models up,

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:18.640
to speed inference up.

00:39:18.640 --> 00:39:20.640
You can use a cheaper model.

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:22.613
You can use this
really cool technique

00:39:22.613 --> 00:39:24.030
called speculative
decoding, where

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:28.800
you use a cheaper model to
scout ahead and generate

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:30.010
multiple tokens.

00:39:30.010 --> 00:39:34.530
And then if these tokens happen
to be good for some definition

00:39:34.530 --> 00:39:38.850
good, you can have the
full model just score

00:39:38.850 --> 00:39:42.630
and accept them all in parallel.

00:39:42.630 --> 00:39:44.790
And then there's a bunch
of systems optimizations

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:46.980
that you can do as well.

00:39:46.980 --> 00:39:49.750
OK, so after the systems--

00:39:49.750 --> 00:39:51.100
oh, OK, Assignment 2.

00:39:51.100 --> 00:39:54.970
So you're going to
implement a kernel.

00:39:54.970 --> 00:39:58.300
You're going to implement
some parallelism.

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:06.820
So data parallel is very
natural, and so we'll do that.

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:09.820
Some of the model
parallelism, like FSDP,

00:40:09.820 --> 00:40:14.600
turns out to be a bit
complicated to do from scratch.

00:40:14.600 --> 00:40:18.100
So we'll do a baby
version of that.

00:40:18.100 --> 00:40:22.510
But I encourage you to learn
and know about the full version.

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:24.440
We'll go over the
full version in class,

00:40:24.440 --> 00:40:29.920
but implementing from scratch
might be a bit too much.

00:40:29.920 --> 00:40:31.600
And then I think
an important thing

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:34.610
is getting in the habit of
always benchmarking and profile.

00:40:34.610 --> 00:40:38.440
I think that's actually probably
the most important thing, is

00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:39.920
that you can implement things.

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:44.590
But unless you have feedback on
how well your implementation is

00:40:44.590 --> 00:40:46.160
going and where the
bottlenecks are,

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:50.170
you're just going
to be flying blind.

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:56.470
OK, so Unit 3 is scaling laws.

00:40:56.470 --> 00:41:00.560
And here, the goal is you want
to do experiments at small scale

00:41:00.560 --> 00:41:04.610
and figure things out, and then
predict the hyperparameters

00:41:04.610 --> 00:41:07.130
and loss at large scale.

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:11.280
So here's a
fundamental question.

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:17.348
So if I give you a FLOPs budget,
what model size should you use?

00:41:17.348 --> 00:41:18.890
If you use a larger
model, that means

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:20.280
you can train on less data.

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:22.738
And if you use a smaller model,
you can train on more data.

00:41:22.738 --> 00:41:24.300
So what's the
right balance here?

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:27.890
And this has been
studied quite extensively

00:41:27.890 --> 00:41:31.800
and figured out by a series of
papers from OpenAI and DeepMind.

00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:34.710
So if you hear the term
Chinchilla optimal,

00:41:34.710 --> 00:41:36.570
this is what this
is referring to.

00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:41.630
And the basic idea is that
for every compute budget

00:41:41.630 --> 00:41:45.620
number of FLOPSs, you can
vary the number of parameters

00:41:45.620 --> 00:41:47.190
of your model.

00:41:47.190 --> 00:41:50.490
OK, and then you measure
how good that model is.

00:41:50.490 --> 00:41:52.580
So for every level
of compute, you

00:41:52.580 --> 00:41:57.300
can get the optimal
parameter count.

00:41:57.300 --> 00:42:04.110
And then what you do is you
can fit a curve to extrapolate

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:08.940
and see if you had,
let's say, 1e22 FLOPs,

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:10.750
what would there be
the parameter size?

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:13.330
And it turns out these
minima, when you plot them,

00:42:13.330 --> 00:42:17.430
it's actually remarkably linear.

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:23.520
Which leads to this very
actually simple but useful rule

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:31.410
of thumb, which is that if you
have a particular model of size

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:34.800
n, if you multiply by 20,
that's the number of tokens

00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:37.090
you should train
on, essentially.

00:42:37.090 --> 00:42:41.490
So that means if I say 1.4
billion parameter model should

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:44.820
be trained on 28 billion tokens.

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:45.710
OK.

00:42:45.710 --> 00:42:48.310
But this doesn't take into
account inference cost.

00:42:48.310 --> 00:42:52.500
This is literally, how can you
train the best model, regardless

00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:54.020
of how big that model is?

00:42:54.020 --> 00:42:55.430
So there's some
limitations here,

00:42:55.430 --> 00:42:57.640
but it's nonetheless
been extremely useful

00:42:57.640 --> 00:42:59.410
for model development.

00:42:59.410 --> 00:43:05.380
So in this assignment, this is
fun, because we define a, quote,

00:43:05.380 --> 00:43:09.490
unquote, "training API," which
you can query with a particular

00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:10.580
set of hyperparameters.

00:43:10.580 --> 00:43:15.050
You specify the architecture
and batch size and so on.

00:43:15.050 --> 00:43:20.830
And we return you a loss that
your decisions will get you.

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:24.790
OK, so your job is you
have a FLOPs budget.

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:26.830
And you're going to
try to figure out

00:43:26.830 --> 00:43:31.130
how to train a bunch of models
and then gather the data.

00:43:31.130 --> 00:43:34.310
You're going to fit a scaling
law to the gathered data.

00:43:34.310 --> 00:43:37.240
And then you're going to
submit your prediction

00:43:37.240 --> 00:43:41.240
on what you would choose
to be the hyperparameters.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:46.715
What model size and so
on at a larger scale.

00:43:46.715 --> 00:43:47.215
OK.

00:43:47.215 --> 00:43:49.630
So this is a case where
you have to be really--

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:51.760
we want to put you
in this position

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:53.910
where there's some stakes.

00:43:53.910 --> 00:43:56.520
I mean, this is not like
burning real compute.

00:43:56.520 --> 00:44:00.380
But once you run out of your
FLOPs budget, that's it.

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:05.060
So you have to be very careful
in terms of how you prioritize

00:44:05.060 --> 00:44:07.012
what experiments to run.

00:44:07.012 --> 00:44:08.720
Which is something
that the frontier labs

00:44:08.720 --> 00:44:10.620
have to do all the time.

00:44:10.620 --> 00:44:12.660
And there will be a
leaderboard for this,

00:44:12.660 --> 00:44:18.500
which is, minimize loss
given the FLOPs budget.

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:20.108
Question?

00:44:20.108 --> 00:44:23.820
So those are things from 2024.

00:44:23.820 --> 00:44:26.150
Yeah, so if we're
working ahead, should

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:29.060
we expect assignments
to change over time,

00:44:29.060 --> 00:44:31.230
or are these going
to be the final--

00:44:31.230 --> 00:44:35.510
Yeah, so the question is that
these links are from 2024.

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:38.330
The rough assignments,
the rough structure

00:44:38.330 --> 00:44:40.500
will be the same for 2025.

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:41.940
There will be some
modifications.

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:43.970
But if you look at these, you
should have a pretty good idea

00:44:43.970 --> 00:44:44.740
of what to expect.

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:53.070
OK, so let's go into data now.

00:44:53.070 --> 00:44:58.030
OK, so up until now, you have
scaling laws, you have systems.

00:44:58.030 --> 00:45:01.600
You have your transformer
implementation and everything.

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:03.670
You're really good to go.

00:45:03.670 --> 00:45:07.980
But data, I would say, is
a really key ingredient

00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:11.350
that I think differentiates
in some sense.

00:45:11.350 --> 00:45:16.290
And the question to ask here is,
what do I want this model to do?

00:45:16.290 --> 00:45:20.820
Right, because what the model
does is completely determine--

00:45:20.820 --> 00:45:24.150
I mean, mostly
determined by the data.

00:45:24.150 --> 00:45:25.870
If I train a
multilingual data, it

00:45:25.870 --> 00:45:27.370
will have multilingual
capabilities.

00:45:27.370 --> 00:45:29.640
If I trained on code, it
will have code capabilities.

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:32.380
And it's very natural.

00:45:32.380 --> 00:45:35.640
And usually data sets
are a conglomeration

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:37.480
of a lot of different pieces.

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:41.710
This is from a pile,
which is four years ago.

00:45:41.710 --> 00:45:44.160
But the same idea,
I think, holds.

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:46.510
You have data from the web.

00:45:46.510 --> 00:45:48.420
This is Common Crawl.

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:51.190
You have StackExchange,
Wikipedia, GitHub,

00:45:51.190 --> 00:45:54.640
and different sources,
which are curated.

00:45:54.640 --> 00:45:57.580
And so in the data
section, we're

00:45:57.580 --> 00:45:59.870
going to start talking
about evaluation,

00:45:59.870 --> 00:46:01.960
which is given a
model, how do you

00:46:01.960 --> 00:46:03.650
evaluate whether it's any good?

00:46:03.650 --> 00:46:08.710
So we're going to talk
about perplexity measures,

00:46:08.710 --> 00:46:11.800
standardized testing, like MMLU.

00:46:11.800 --> 00:46:15.280
If you have models that
generate utterances

00:46:15.280 --> 00:46:18.310
for instruction following,
how do you evaluate that?

00:46:18.310 --> 00:46:23.500
There's also decisions about,
if you can ensemble or do

00:46:23.500 --> 00:46:26.530
train of thought at
test time, how does that

00:46:26.530 --> 00:46:28.450
affect your evaluation?

00:46:28.450 --> 00:46:34.270
And then you can talk about
entire systems, evaluation

00:46:34.270 --> 00:46:36.220
of entire system, not
just the language model,

00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:38.080
because language
models often get

00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:43.480
these days plugged into some
agentic system or something.

00:46:43.480 --> 00:46:48.060
OK, so now after
establishing evaluation,

00:46:48.060 --> 00:46:50.170
let's look at data curation.

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:52.680
So this is, I think,
an important point

00:46:52.680 --> 00:46:53.950
that people don't realize.

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:57.360
I often hear people
say, oh, we're training

00:46:57.360 --> 00:46:59.710
the model on the internet.

00:46:59.710 --> 00:47:01.815
This just doesn't make sense.

00:47:01.815 --> 00:47:06.070
Right, data doesn't
just fall from the sky

00:47:06.070 --> 00:47:11.400
and there's the internet that
you can pipe into your model.

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:16.710
Data has to always be
actively acquired somehow.

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:22.260
So even just as an example of--

00:47:22.260 --> 00:47:25.830
I always tell people,
look at the data.

00:47:25.830 --> 00:47:27.520
And so let's look at some data.

00:47:27.520 --> 00:47:32.800
So this is some
Common Crawl data.

00:47:32.800 --> 00:47:34.690
I'm going to take 10 documents.

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:37.440
And I think
hopefully, this works.

00:47:37.440 --> 00:47:37.960
OK.

00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:41.340
I think the rendering
is off, but you

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:50.770
can see this is a random
sample of Common Crawl.

00:47:50.770 --> 00:48:00.020
And you can see that this is
maybe not exactly the data.

00:48:00.020 --> 00:48:01.850
Oh, here's some
actually real text here.

00:48:01.850 --> 00:48:03.100
OK, that's cool.

00:48:03.100 --> 00:48:05.832
But if you look at most
of Common Crawl, aside--

00:48:05.832 --> 00:48:07.040
this is a different language.

00:48:07.040 --> 00:48:09.950
But you can also see this
is very spammy sites.

00:48:09.950 --> 00:48:15.650
And you'll quickly realize that
a lot of the web is just trash.

00:48:15.650 --> 00:48:19.940
And so-- well, OK, maybe
that's not that's surprising,

00:48:19.940 --> 00:48:22.490
but it's more trash than
you would actually expect,

00:48:22.490 --> 00:48:23.770
I promise.

00:48:23.770 --> 00:48:27.760
So what I'm saying is
that there's a lot of work

00:48:27.760 --> 00:48:29.690
that needs to happen in data.

00:48:29.690 --> 00:48:31.550
So you can crawl the internet.

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:36.550
You can take books,
archives of papers, GitHub.

00:48:36.550 --> 00:48:40.330
And there's actually a lot of
processing that needs to happen.

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:43.870
There's also legal questions
about what data you can

00:48:43.870 --> 00:48:46.190
train on, which we'll touch on.

00:48:46.190 --> 00:48:48.710
Nowadays, a lot
of frontier models

00:48:48.710 --> 00:48:50.900
have to actually buy data.

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:55.220
Because the data on the internet
that's publicly accessible

00:48:55.220 --> 00:49:02.120
actually turns out to be a bit
limited for the really frontier

00:49:02.120 --> 00:49:03.240
performance.

00:49:03.240 --> 00:49:05.420
And also, I think it's
important to remember

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:09.190
that this data that's scraped,
it's not actually text, right.

00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:11.910
First of all, it's
HTML or it's PDFs.

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:14.220
Or in the case of code,
it's just directories.

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:17.960
So there has to be an explicit
process that takes this data

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:20.310
and turns it into text.

00:49:20.310 --> 00:49:24.770
OK, so we're going to talk about
the transformation from HTML

00:49:24.770 --> 00:49:27.260
to text.

00:49:27.260 --> 00:49:30.890
And this is going to
be a lossy process.

00:49:30.890 --> 00:49:35.780
So the trick is, how can you
preserve the content and some

00:49:35.780 --> 00:49:43.500
of the structure without
basically just having an HTML?

00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:46.770
Filtering, as you
could surmise, is

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:49.650
going to be very important, both
for getting high quality data

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:52.080
but also removing
harmful content.

00:49:52.080 --> 00:49:55.060
Generally, people train
classifiers to do this.

00:49:55.060 --> 00:49:58.870
Deduplication is also
an important step,

00:49:58.870 --> 00:50:00.100
which we'll talk about.

00:50:00.100 --> 00:50:02.860
OK, so Assignment 4
is all about data.

00:50:02.860 --> 00:50:07.560
We're going to give you the
raw Common Crawl dump so you

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:10.060
can see just how bad it is.

00:50:10.060 --> 00:50:13.600
And you're going to train
classifiers, dedupe.

00:50:13.600 --> 00:50:15.600
And then there's going
to be a leaderboard where

00:50:15.600 --> 00:50:19.110
you're going to try to
minimize perplexity given

00:50:19.110 --> 00:50:20.820
your token budget.

00:50:20.820 --> 00:50:24.370
So now you have the data.

00:50:24.370 --> 00:50:28.090
You've done this-- build
all your fancy kernels.

00:50:28.090 --> 00:50:29.890
Now you can really train models.

00:50:29.890 --> 00:50:32.010
But at this point,
what you'll get

00:50:32.010 --> 00:50:36.960
is a model that can
complete the next token.

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:37.900
Right.

00:50:37.900 --> 00:50:40.990
And this is called
essentially a base model.

00:50:40.990 --> 00:50:43.910
And I think about it as a model
that has a lot of raw potential.

00:50:43.910 --> 00:50:47.750
But it needs to be aligned
or modified in some way.

00:50:47.750 --> 00:50:50.060
And alignment is a process
of making it useful.

00:50:50.060 --> 00:50:55.250
So alignment captures a
lot of different things.

00:50:55.250 --> 00:50:58.120
But three things I
think it captures

00:50:58.120 --> 00:50:59.740
is that you want
to get the language

00:50:59.740 --> 00:51:01.660
model to follow instructions.

00:51:01.660 --> 00:51:04.120
Right, complete the next
token is not necessarily

00:51:04.120 --> 00:51:05.540
following the instruction.

00:51:05.540 --> 00:51:07.360
It will just complete
the instruction

00:51:07.360 --> 00:51:11.020
or whatever it thinks will
follow the instruction.

00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:16.250
You get to here, specify
the style of the generation.

00:51:16.250 --> 00:51:18.470
Whether you want it
to be long or short,

00:51:18.470 --> 00:51:19.700
whether you want bullets.

00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:24.550
Whether you want it to be
witty or have SaaS or not.

00:51:24.550 --> 00:51:29.210
And when you play with
ChatGPT versus Grok,

00:51:29.210 --> 00:51:32.170
you'll see that there's
different alignment that

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:33.560
has happened.

00:51:33.560 --> 00:51:35.710
And then also safety.

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:37.630
One important thing
is for these models

00:51:37.630 --> 00:51:41.430
to be able to refuse
answers that can be harmful.

00:51:41.430 --> 00:51:43.740
So that's where
alignment also kicks in.

00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:47.520
So there's generally
two phases of alignment.

00:51:47.520 --> 00:51:49.410
There's supervised fine tuning.

00:51:49.410 --> 00:51:51.620
And here, the goal is--

00:51:51.620 --> 00:51:53.160
I mean, it's very simple.

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:59.960
You basically gather a set
of user assistant pairs.

00:51:59.960 --> 00:52:01.760
So prompt response pairs.

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:05.840
And then you do
supervised learning.

00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:06.740
OK.

00:52:06.740 --> 00:52:09.950
And the idea here is that
the base model already

00:52:09.950 --> 00:52:12.600
has the raw potential.

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:18.120
So just fine tuning it on a
few examples is sufficient.

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:21.150
Of course, the more examples you
have, the better the results.

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:23.300
But there's papers
like this one that

00:52:23.300 --> 00:52:26.420
shows even a thousand
examples suffices

00:52:26.420 --> 00:52:29.090
to give you instruction
following capabilities

00:52:29.090 --> 00:52:31.280
from the good base model.

00:52:31.280 --> 00:52:34.260
OK, so this part is
actually very simple.

00:52:34.260 --> 00:52:37.310
And it's not that different
from pre-training,

00:52:37.310 --> 00:52:39.150
because it's just
you're given text

00:52:39.150 --> 00:52:43.890
and you just maximize the
probability of the text.

00:52:43.890 --> 00:52:46.380
So the second part is
a bit more interesting

00:52:46.380 --> 00:52:48.670
from an algorithmic perspective.

00:52:48.670 --> 00:52:52.300
So the idea here is that
even with an SFT phase,

00:52:52.300 --> 00:52:55.780
you will have a decent model.

00:52:55.780 --> 00:52:57.460
And now, how do you improve it?

00:52:57.460 --> 00:52:59.060
Well, you can gather
more SFT data,

00:52:59.060 --> 00:53:00.810
but that can be very
expensive because you

00:53:00.810 --> 00:53:04.540
have to have someone sit
down and annotate data.

00:53:04.540 --> 00:53:09.120
So the goal of
learning from feedback

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:13.650
is that you can leverage
lighter forms of annotation

00:53:13.650 --> 00:53:16.720
and have the algorithms
do a bit more work.

00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:20.590
OK, so one type of data you can
learn from is preference data.

00:53:20.590 --> 00:53:24.450
So this is where you
generate multiple responses

00:53:24.450 --> 00:53:26.890
from a model to a given
prompt, like A or B.

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:30.370
And the user rates
whether A or B is better.

00:53:30.370 --> 00:53:34.710
And so the data might
look like it generates--

00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:36.850
what's the best way to
train a language model?

00:53:36.850 --> 00:53:39.200
Use a large data set or
use a small data set.

00:53:39.200 --> 00:53:41.980
And of course, the
answer should be A. So

00:53:41.980 --> 00:53:46.340
that is a unit of
expressing preferences.

00:53:46.340 --> 00:53:50.420
Another type of supervision you
could have is using verifiers.

00:53:50.420 --> 00:53:52.300
So for some domains,
you're lucky enough

00:53:52.300 --> 00:53:56.030
to have a formal verifier,
like for math or code.

00:53:56.030 --> 00:53:59.020
Or you can use learned
verifiers where

00:53:59.020 --> 00:54:07.070
you train an actual language
model to rate the response.

00:54:07.070 --> 00:54:10.750
And of course, this relates
to evaluation again.

00:54:10.750 --> 00:54:13.210
Algorithms.

00:54:13.210 --> 00:54:15.440
We're in the realm of
reinforcement learning.

00:54:15.440 --> 00:54:18.830
So one of the
earliest algorithms

00:54:18.830 --> 00:54:23.320
that was developed that was
applied to instruction tuning

00:54:23.320 --> 00:54:29.740
models was PPO, Proximal
Policy Optimization.

00:54:29.740 --> 00:54:32.140
It turns out that if you
just have preference data,

00:54:32.140 --> 00:54:34.550
there's a much simpler
algorithm called

00:54:34.550 --> 00:54:37.340
DPO that works really well.

00:54:37.340 --> 00:54:41.310
But in general, if you wanted
to learn from verifier's data,

00:54:41.310 --> 00:54:43.050
you have to-- it's
not preference data,

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:45.360
so you have to embrace RL fully.

00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:50.480
And there's this
method, which we'll

00:54:50.480 --> 00:54:54.290
do in this class, which is
called group relative preference

00:54:54.290 --> 00:54:57.410
optimization, which simplifies
PPO and makes it more efficient

00:54:57.410 --> 00:55:00.800
by removing the value function
developed by DeepSeek, which

00:55:00.800 --> 00:55:03.950
seems to work pretty well.

00:55:03.950 --> 00:55:09.870
OK, so Assignment 5 implements
supervised tuning, DPO and GRPO.

00:55:09.870 --> 00:55:12.980
And of course, evaluate.

00:55:12.980 --> 00:55:13.480
Question.

00:55:16.190 --> 00:55:19.850
I think a quote from the course
[INAUDIBLE] about Assignment 1--

00:55:19.850 --> 00:55:22.280
did people have similar things
to say about Assignments 2

00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:23.550
through 5?

00:55:23.550 --> 00:55:27.660
Yeah, the question is Assignment
1 seems a bit daunting.

00:55:27.660 --> 00:55:28.890
What about the other ones?

00:55:28.890 --> 00:55:32.150
I would say that Assignment
1 and 2 are definitely

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:35.100
the most heavy and hardest.

00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:39.580
Assignment 3 is a bit
more of a breather.

00:55:39.580 --> 00:55:42.390
And Assignment 4 and
5, at least last year,

00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:47.250
were, I would say, a notch
below Assignment 1 or 2.

00:55:47.250 --> 00:55:49.080
Although, I don't
know, it depends on--

00:55:49.080 --> 00:55:52.180
we haven't fully worked out
the details for this year.

00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:58.530
Yeah, it does get better.

00:55:58.530 --> 00:56:05.160
OK, so just to recap of
the different pieces here.

00:56:05.160 --> 00:56:08.220
Remember, efficiency is
this driving principle.

00:56:08.220 --> 00:56:12.930
And there's a bunch of
different design decisions.

00:56:12.930 --> 00:56:16.518
I think if you view
efficiency, everything

00:56:16.518 --> 00:56:18.810
through the lens of efficiency,
I think a lot of things

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:21.330
make sense.

00:56:21.330 --> 00:56:25.050
And importantly,
I think we are--

00:56:25.050 --> 00:56:26.790
it's worth pointing
out there, we

00:56:26.790 --> 00:56:30.370
are currently in this
compute constrained regime,

00:56:30.370 --> 00:56:32.070
at least this class
and most people who

00:56:32.070 --> 00:56:33.920
are somewhat GPU poor.

00:56:33.920 --> 00:56:37.160
So we have a lot of data, but
we don't have that much compute.

00:56:37.160 --> 00:56:40.480
And so these design decisions
will reflect squeezing the most

00:56:40.480 --> 00:56:41.390
out of the hardware.

00:56:41.390 --> 00:56:43.190
So for example, data processing.

00:56:43.190 --> 00:56:45.122
We're filtering
fairly aggressively

00:56:45.122 --> 00:56:47.080
because we don't want to
waste precious compute

00:56:47.080 --> 00:56:49.600
on bad or irrelevant data.

00:56:49.600 --> 00:56:51.370
Tokenization.

00:56:51.370 --> 00:56:54.807
It's nice to have
a model over bytes.

00:56:54.807 --> 00:56:55.640
That's very elegant.

00:56:55.640 --> 00:56:58.660
But it's very compute
inefficient with today's model

00:56:58.660 --> 00:56:59.540
architectures.

00:56:59.540 --> 00:57:03.490
So we have to do tokenization
as an efficiency gain.

00:57:03.490 --> 00:57:06.880
Model architecture, there are
a lot of design decisions there

00:57:06.880 --> 00:57:11.630
that are essentially motivated
by efficiency training.

00:57:11.630 --> 00:57:14.410
I think the fact that most
of what we're going to do

00:57:14.410 --> 00:57:16.130
is just a single epoch.

00:57:16.130 --> 00:57:18.590
This is clearly
we're in a hurry.

00:57:18.590 --> 00:57:22.090
We just need to see more data as
opposed to spend a lot of time

00:57:22.090 --> 00:57:23.870
on any given data point.

00:57:23.870 --> 00:57:26.510
Scaling laws is completely
about efficiency.

00:57:26.510 --> 00:57:30.010
We use less compute to figure
out the hyperparameters.

00:57:30.010 --> 00:57:35.850
And alignment is maybe
a little bit different.

00:57:35.850 --> 00:57:41.900
But the connection to efficiency
is that if you can put resources

00:57:41.900 --> 00:57:44.150
into alignment,
then you actually

00:57:44.150 --> 00:57:49.130
require smaller base models.

00:57:49.130 --> 00:57:50.534
OK.

00:57:50.534 --> 00:57:52.620
So there's two paths.

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:55.470
If your use case
is fairly narrow,

00:57:55.470 --> 00:57:57.060
you can probably
use a smaller model.

00:57:57.060 --> 00:58:00.450
You align it or fine tune
it, and you can do well.

00:58:00.450 --> 00:58:03.000
But if your use
cases are very broad,

00:58:03.000 --> 00:58:04.970
then there might
not be a substitute

00:58:04.970 --> 00:58:07.520
for training a big model.

00:58:07.520 --> 00:58:09.210
So that's today.

00:58:09.210 --> 00:58:13.970
So increasingly now, at
least for Frontier Labs,

00:58:13.970 --> 00:58:16.110
they're becoming
data constrained.

00:58:16.110 --> 00:58:18.260
Which is interesting
because I think

00:58:18.260 --> 00:58:22.890
that the design decisions will
presumably completely change.

00:58:22.890 --> 00:58:25.470
Well, I mean, compute
will always be important,

00:58:25.470 --> 00:58:27.540
but I think the design
decisions will change.

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:33.060
For example, taking one
epoch of your data, I think,

00:58:33.060 --> 00:58:34.760
doesn't really make sense.

00:58:34.760 --> 00:58:36.510
If you have more
compute, why wouldn't you

00:58:36.510 --> 00:58:39.670
take more epochs at least,
or do something smarter?

00:58:39.670 --> 00:58:44.370
Or maybe there will be different
architectures, for example.

00:58:44.370 --> 00:58:47.640
Because the transformer
was really motivated by

00:58:47.640 --> 00:58:50.160
compute efficiency.

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.360
So that's something to ponder.

00:58:52.360 --> 00:58:53.830
Still, it's about efficiency.

00:58:53.830 --> 00:58:56.750
But the design decisions
reflect what regime you're in.

00:58:59.790 --> 00:59:00.738
OK.

00:59:00.738 --> 00:59:08.010
So now I'm going to dive
into the first unit.

00:59:08.010 --> 00:59:09.976
Before that, any questions?

00:59:15.330 --> 00:59:18.350
Do you have a Slack or Ed?

00:59:18.350 --> 00:59:20.100
The question is, do
we have a Slack or Ed?

00:59:20.100 --> 00:59:21.520
We will have a Slack.

00:59:21.520 --> 00:59:25.560
We'll send out details
after this class.

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:26.167
Yeah.

00:59:26.167 --> 00:59:28.250
Will students auditing the
course also have access

00:59:28.250 --> 00:59:30.350
to the same material?

00:59:30.350 --> 00:59:32.000
The question is,
students auditing

00:59:32.000 --> 00:59:38.940
the class will have access
to all the online materials,

00:59:38.940 --> 00:59:39.750
assignments.

00:59:39.750 --> 00:59:43.340
And we'll give you
