01:00:22.280 --> 01:00:24.385
of what grading looks like.

01:00:28.560 --> 01:00:31.960
OK, let's jump
into tokenization.

01:00:31.960 --> 01:00:35.520
OK, so Andrej Karpathy
has this really nice video

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:36.670
on tokenization.

01:00:36.670 --> 01:00:41.730
And in general, he makes a lot
of these videos on that actually

01:00:41.730 --> 01:00:43.000
inspired a lot of this class.

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:45.660
How you can build
things from scratch.

01:00:45.660 --> 01:00:49.590
So you should go check
out some of his videos.

01:00:49.590 --> 01:00:53.670
So tokenization, as
we talked about it,

01:00:53.670 --> 01:00:56.580
is the process of taking
raw text, which is generally

01:00:56.580 --> 01:01:00.780
represented as Unicode
strings, and turning it

01:01:00.780 --> 01:01:04.600
into a set of
integers essentially.

01:01:04.600 --> 01:01:09.090
And where each integer
represents a token.

01:01:09.090 --> 01:01:12.270
OK, so we need a procedure
that encodes strings to tokens

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:16.350
and decodes them
back into strings.

01:01:16.350 --> 01:01:19.680
And the vocabulary size is
just the number of values

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:24.570
that a token take on the number
of the range of the integers.

01:01:24.570 --> 01:01:29.150
So just to give you an example
of how tokenizers work,

01:01:29.150 --> 01:01:32.390
let's play around with
this really nice website,

01:01:32.390 --> 01:01:35.350
which allows you to look at
different tokenizers and just

01:01:35.350 --> 01:01:39.640
type in something like hello.

01:01:39.640 --> 01:01:43.660
Hello, or whatever.

01:01:43.660 --> 01:01:48.310
Maybe I'll do this.

01:01:48.310 --> 01:01:52.940
And one thing it does is it
shows you the list of integers.

01:01:52.940 --> 01:01:54.320
This is the output of tokenizer.

01:01:54.320 --> 01:01:58.090
It also nicely maps
out the decomposition

01:01:58.090 --> 01:02:02.260
of the original string
into a bunch of segments.

01:02:02.260 --> 01:02:04.670
And a few things to note.

01:02:04.670 --> 01:02:08.690
First of all, the space
is part of a token.

01:02:08.690 --> 01:02:12.530
So unlike classical NLP where
the space just disappears,

01:02:12.530 --> 01:02:14.780
everything is accounted for.

01:02:14.780 --> 01:02:19.540
These are meant to be reversible
operations, tokenization.

01:02:19.540 --> 01:02:23.630
And by convention,
for whatever reason,

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:27.890
the space is usually
preceding the token.

01:02:27.890 --> 01:02:32.480
Also notice that hello is a
completely different token

01:02:32.480 --> 01:02:40.100
than space hello, which might
make you a little bit squeamish.

01:02:40.100 --> 01:02:44.160
And it can cause problems,
but that's just how it is.

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:44.810
Question?

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:47.870
Can I ask, is the space being
leading instead of trailing

01:02:47.870 --> 01:02:51.800
intentional, or is it just an
artifact of the BPE process?

01:02:51.800 --> 01:02:54.590
So the question
is, is the spacing

01:02:54.590 --> 01:02:57.620
before intentional or not?

01:02:57.620 --> 01:03:02.330
So in the BPE process
that we'll talk about,

01:03:02.330 --> 01:03:09.060
you're actually pre-tokenized,
and then you tokenize each part.

01:03:09.060 --> 01:03:12.330
And I think the pre-tokenizer
does put the space in the front.

01:03:12.330 --> 01:03:14.520
So it is built
into the algorithm.

01:03:14.520 --> 01:03:15.720
You could put it at the end.

01:03:15.720 --> 01:03:18.230
But I think it probably
makes more sense

01:03:18.230 --> 01:03:20.970
to put it in the beginning.

01:03:20.970 --> 01:03:23.950
But I actually don't--

01:03:23.950 --> 01:03:27.180
well, I guess it
could go either way.

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:29.100
If it makes sense.

01:03:29.100 --> 01:03:32.550
OK, so then if you
look at numbers,

01:03:32.550 --> 01:03:35.310
you see that the
numbers are chopped down

01:03:35.310 --> 01:03:39.480
into different pieces.

01:03:39.480 --> 01:03:43.297
It's a little bit interesting
that it's left to right.

01:03:43.297 --> 01:03:45.630
So it's definitely not grouping
by thousands or anything

01:03:45.630 --> 01:03:47.100
semantic.

01:03:47.100 --> 01:03:49.290
But anyway, I encourage
you to play with it

01:03:49.290 --> 01:03:54.220
and get a sense of what these
existing tokenizers look like.

01:03:54.220 --> 01:03:59.700
So this is the tokenizer
for GPT 4.0, for example.

01:03:59.700 --> 01:04:03.720
So there are some
observations that we made.

01:04:03.720 --> 01:04:06.900
So if you look at
the GPT-2 tokenizer,

01:04:06.900 --> 01:04:10.890
which will use this
as a reference--

01:04:10.890 --> 01:04:12.995
OK, let me see if I can--

01:04:16.020 --> 01:04:17.940
hopefully this is--
let me know if this is

01:04:17.940 --> 01:04:19.740
getting too small in the back.

01:04:19.740 --> 01:04:22.390
You can take a string.

01:04:22.390 --> 01:04:27.350
If you apply the GPT-2
tokenizer, you get your indices.

01:04:27.350 --> 01:04:29.420
So it maps strings to indices.

01:04:29.420 --> 01:04:33.560
And then you can decode
to get back the string.

01:04:33.560 --> 01:04:36.190
And this is just a
sanity check to make sure

01:04:36.190 --> 01:04:40.300
that you actually round trips.

01:04:40.300 --> 01:04:42.820
Another thing that I guess
interesting to look at

01:04:42.820 --> 01:04:44.950
is this compression
ratio, which is

01:04:44.950 --> 01:04:48.280
if you look at the
number of bytes divided

01:04:48.280 --> 01:04:49.550
by the number of tokens.

01:04:49.550 --> 01:04:53.240
So how many bytes are
represented by a token?

01:04:53.240 --> 01:04:56.835
And the answer here is 1.6.

01:04:56.835 --> 01:05:02.050
OK, so every token
represents 1.6 bytes of data.

01:05:02.050 --> 01:05:06.610
OK, so that's just a GPT
tokenizer that OpenAI trained.

01:05:06.610 --> 01:05:12.020
To motivate BPE, I want to go
through a sequence of attempts.

01:05:12.020 --> 01:05:14.030
So suppose you wanted
to do tokenization.

01:05:14.030 --> 01:05:17.380
What would be the
simplest thing?

01:05:17.380 --> 01:05:20.250
The simplest thing is probably
character-based tokenization.

01:05:20.250 --> 01:05:24.180
A Unicode string is a sequence
of Unicode characters.

01:05:24.180 --> 01:05:27.410
And each character can be
converted into an integer

01:05:27.410 --> 01:05:29.240
and called a code point.

01:05:29.240 --> 01:05:32.390
OK, so A maps to 97.

01:05:32.390 --> 01:05:37.140
The world emoji maps to 127,757.

01:05:37.140 --> 01:05:39.570
And you can see that
it converts back.

01:05:39.570 --> 01:05:41.660
OK, so you can
define a tokenizer,

01:05:41.660 --> 01:05:51.200
which simply maps each
character into a code point.

01:05:51.200 --> 01:05:53.200
OK.

01:05:53.200 --> 01:05:58.130
So what's one problem with this?

01:05:58.130 --> 01:05:59.000
Yeah?

01:05:59.000 --> 01:06:00.620
Compression ratio is one.

01:06:00.620 --> 01:06:03.110
The compression ratio is one.

01:06:03.110 --> 01:06:06.800
So that's-- well, actually, the
compression ratio is not quite

01:06:06.800 --> 01:06:09.680
one because a character
is not a byte.

01:06:09.680 --> 01:06:12.710
But it's maybe not
as good as you want.

01:06:12.710 --> 01:06:16.220
One problem with that, if
you look at some code points,

01:06:16.220 --> 01:06:19.170
they're actually
really large, right.

01:06:19.170 --> 01:06:25.440
So you're basically allocating
each one slot in your vocabulary

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:27.670
for every character uniformly.

01:06:27.670 --> 01:06:30.850
And some characters appear way
more frequently than others.

01:06:30.850 --> 01:06:36.870
So this is not a very
effective use of your budget.

01:06:36.870 --> 01:06:37.830
OK.

01:06:37.830 --> 01:06:41.050
So the vocabulary size is huge.

01:06:41.050 --> 01:06:43.530
I mean, the vocabulary
size being 127

01:06:43.530 --> 01:06:45.190
is actually a big deal.

01:06:45.190 --> 01:06:49.570
But the bigger problem is
that some characters are rare,

01:06:49.570 --> 01:06:53.731
and this is inefficient
use of the vocab.

01:06:53.731 --> 01:06:59.700
OK, so the compression
ratio is 1.5 in this case,

01:06:59.700 --> 01:07:02.310
because it's the tokens--

01:07:02.310 --> 01:07:04.470
sorry, the number
of bytes per token.

01:07:04.470 --> 01:07:09.270
And a character can
be multiple bytes.

01:07:09.270 --> 01:07:13.800
OK, so that was a
very naive approach.

01:07:13.800 --> 01:07:16.930
On the other hand, you can
do byte-based tokenization.

01:07:16.930 --> 01:07:19.870
OK, so Unicode strings
can be represented

01:07:19.870 --> 01:07:22.000
as a sequence of bytes.

01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:30.500
Because every string can just
be converted into bytes OK,

01:07:30.500 --> 01:07:35.000
so A is already just one byte.

01:07:35.000 --> 01:07:39.950
But some characters take
up as many as four bytes.

01:07:39.950 --> 01:07:43.700
And this is using the
UTF-8 encoding of Unicode.

01:07:43.700 --> 01:07:46.240
There's other encodings, but
this is the most common one

01:07:46.240 --> 01:07:48.910
that's dynamic.

01:07:48.910 --> 01:07:56.080
So let's just convert everything
into bytes and see what happens.

01:07:56.080 --> 01:07:59.440
So if you do it into
bytes, now all the indices

01:07:59.440 --> 01:08:01.810
are between 0 and 256,
because there are only

01:08:01.810 --> 01:08:06.310
256 possible values for
a byte by definition.

01:08:06.310 --> 01:08:09.130
So your vocabulary
is very small.

01:08:09.130 --> 01:08:10.610
And each byte is--

01:08:10.610 --> 01:08:14.940
I guess not all bytes are
equally used, but it's not--

01:08:14.940 --> 01:08:18.274
you don't have that
many sparsity problems.

01:08:18.274 --> 01:08:20.274
But what's the problem
with byte-based encoding?

01:08:23.844 --> 01:08:25.250
Long sequences.

01:08:25.250 --> 01:08:28.010
Yeah, long sequences.

01:08:28.010 --> 01:08:30.899
I mean, in some ways, I really
wish byte coding would work.

01:08:30.899 --> 01:08:33.060
It's the most elegant thing.

01:08:33.060 --> 01:08:36.300
But you have long sequences.

01:08:36.300 --> 01:08:40.220
Your compression ratio
is 1, one byte per token.

01:08:40.220 --> 01:08:42.458
And this is just terrible.

01:08:42.458 --> 01:08:44.000
A compression ratio
of 1 is terrible,

01:08:44.000 --> 01:08:47.040
because your sequences
will be really long.

01:08:47.040 --> 01:08:50.040
Attention is quadratic naively
in the sequence length.

01:08:50.040 --> 01:08:54.929
So you're just going to have a
bad time in terms of efficiency.

01:08:54.929 --> 01:08:55.429
OK.

01:08:55.429 --> 01:08:59.479
So that wasn't really good.

01:08:59.479 --> 01:09:03.470
So now the thing that
you might think about

01:09:03.470 --> 01:09:07.580
is, well, maybe we have
to be adaptive here.

01:09:07.580 --> 01:09:11.460
Right, we can't allocate a
character or a byte per token.

01:09:11.460 --> 01:09:14.160
But maybe some tokens can
represent lots of bytes

01:09:14.160 --> 01:09:16.390
and some tokens can
represent few bytes.

01:09:16.390 --> 01:09:19.510
So one way to do this is
word-based tokenization.

01:09:19.510 --> 01:09:23.590
And this is something that was
actually very classic in NLP.

01:09:23.590 --> 01:09:25.180
OK, so here's a string.

01:09:25.180 --> 01:09:33.060
And you can just split it
into a sequence of segments.

01:09:33.060 --> 01:09:33.640
OK.

01:09:33.640 --> 01:09:35.880
And you can call
each of these tokens.

01:09:35.880 --> 01:09:38.700
So you just use a
regular expression.

01:09:38.700 --> 01:09:40.740
Here's a different
regular expression

01:09:40.740 --> 01:09:44.100
that GPT-2 uses to pre-tokenize.

01:09:44.100 --> 01:09:53.040
And it just splits your string
into a sequence of strings.

01:09:53.040 --> 01:09:56.610
So and then what you
do with each segment

01:09:56.610 --> 01:09:58.710
is you assign each of
these to an integer,

01:09:58.710 --> 01:10:01.200
and then you're done.

01:10:01.200 --> 01:10:02.436
OK.

01:10:02.436 --> 01:10:04.250
So what's the problem with this?

01:10:07.582 --> 01:10:08.970
Vocabulary size.

01:10:08.970 --> 01:10:11.590
Yeah, so the problem is
that your vocabulary size

01:10:11.590 --> 01:10:13.450
is unbounded.

01:10:13.450 --> 01:10:14.150
Or not.

01:10:14.150 --> 01:10:18.175
Maybe not quite unbounded, but
you don't know how big it is.

01:10:18.175 --> 01:10:20.660
Right, because on
a given new input,

01:10:20.660 --> 01:10:25.970
you might get a segment that
just you've never seen before.

01:10:25.970 --> 01:10:28.510
And that's actually
a big problem.

01:10:28.510 --> 01:10:31.210
Word base is a really
big pain in the butt,

01:10:31.210 --> 01:10:34.850
because some real
words are rare.

01:10:34.850 --> 01:10:39.550
And actually, it's
really annoying

01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:43.750
because new words have to
receive this UNC token.

01:10:43.750 --> 01:10:48.340
And if you're not careful about
how you compute the perplexity,

01:10:48.340 --> 01:10:51.490
then you just mess up.

01:10:51.490 --> 01:10:55.060
So word based isn't--

01:10:55.060 --> 01:10:58.840
I think it captures the right
intuition of adaptivity.

01:10:58.840 --> 01:11:02.320
But it's not exactly
what we want here.

01:11:02.320 --> 01:11:06.460
So here, we're finally
going to talk about the BPE

01:11:06.460 --> 01:11:08.770
encoding or Byte Pair Encoding.

01:11:08.770 --> 01:11:11.850
So this was actually
a very old algorithm

01:11:11.850 --> 01:11:16.710
developed by Phillip Gage
in '94 for data compression.

01:11:16.710 --> 01:11:19.650
And it was first
introduced into NLP

01:11:19.650 --> 01:11:22.390
for neural machine translation.

01:11:22.390 --> 01:11:25.950
So before, papers that
did machine translation

01:11:25.950 --> 01:11:30.300
or any-- basically, all NLP
used word based tokenization.

01:11:30.300 --> 01:11:33.820
And again, word
base with a pain.

01:11:33.820 --> 01:11:37.950
So this paper pioneered
this idea, well, we

01:11:37.950 --> 01:11:40.990
can use this nice
algorithm form 94,

01:11:40.990 --> 01:11:45.040
and we can just make the
tokenization round trip.

01:11:45.040 --> 01:11:48.780
And we don't have to deal with
UNCs or any of that stuff.

01:11:48.780 --> 01:11:53.310
And then finally, this entered
the language modeling era

01:11:53.310 --> 01:12:01.452
through GPT-2, which was trained
on using the BPE tokenizer.

01:12:01.452 --> 01:12:04.650
OK, so the basic idea
is instead of defining

01:12:04.650 --> 01:12:09.130
some preconceived notion
of how to split it up,

01:12:09.130 --> 01:12:11.860
we're going to train the
tokenizer on raw text.

01:12:11.860 --> 01:12:14.740
That's the basic
insight, if you will.

01:12:14.740 --> 01:12:18.840
And so organically,
common sequences

01:12:18.840 --> 01:12:21.390
that span multiple
characters, we're

01:12:21.390 --> 01:12:23.430
going to try to
represent as one token.

01:12:23.430 --> 01:12:25.530
And rare sequences
are going to be

01:12:25.530 --> 01:12:29.010
represented by multiple tokens.

01:12:29.010 --> 01:12:33.070
There's a slight detail,
which is for efficiency,

01:12:33.070 --> 01:12:37.320
the GPT-2 paper uses word based
tokenizer as a pre-processing

01:12:37.320 --> 01:12:39.270
to break it up into
segments, and then runs BP

01:12:39.270 --> 01:12:41.395
on each of the segments,
which is what you're going

01:12:41.395 --> 01:12:43.950
to do in this class as well.

01:12:43.950 --> 01:12:46.450
The algorithm BP is
actually very simple.

01:12:46.450 --> 01:12:49.320
So we first convert the
string into a sequence

01:12:49.320 --> 01:12:51.420
of bytes, which we
already did when we talked

01:12:51.420 --> 01:12:52.870
about byte-based tokenization.

01:12:52.870 --> 01:12:54.450
And now we're going
to successively

01:12:54.450 --> 01:12:58.880
merge the most common pair of
adjacent tokens over and over

01:12:58.880 --> 01:12:59.380
again.

01:12:59.380 --> 01:13:02.530
So the intuition is that if a
pair of tokens shows up a lot,

01:13:02.530 --> 01:13:05.110
then we're going to
compress it into one token.

01:13:05.110 --> 01:13:07.670
We're just going to
dedicate space for that.

01:13:07.670 --> 01:13:10.530
OK, so let's walk through what
this algorithm looks like.

01:13:10.530 --> 01:13:13.640
So we're going to use this
cat and hat as an example.

01:13:13.640 --> 01:13:20.970
And we're going to convert this
into a sequence of integers.

01:13:20.970 --> 01:13:23.450
These are the bytes.

01:13:23.450 --> 01:13:27.330
And then we're going to keep
track of what we've merged.

01:13:27.330 --> 01:13:32.630
So remember, merges is a
map from two integers which

01:13:32.630 --> 01:13:36.510
can represent bytes or
other pre-existing tokens.

01:13:36.510 --> 01:13:39.050
And we're going to
create a new token.

01:13:39.050 --> 01:13:43.850
And the vocab is just
going to be a handy way

01:13:43.850 --> 01:13:46.205
to represent the index to bytes.

01:13:49.190 --> 01:13:51.390
The BPE algorithm, I
mean, it's very simple,

01:13:51.390 --> 01:13:53.515
so I'm just actually going
to run through the code.

01:13:53.515 --> 01:13:56.130
You're going to do this
num merges of times.

01:13:56.130 --> 01:13:58.230
So num merges is
3, in this case.

01:13:58.230 --> 01:14:02.480
We're going to first count
up the number of occurrences

01:14:02.480 --> 01:14:03.600
of pairs of bytes.

01:14:03.600 --> 01:14:06.340
So hopefully, this
doesn't become too small.

01:14:06.340 --> 01:14:10.980
So we're going to just
step through this sequence,

01:14:10.980 --> 01:14:12.640
and we're going to see that--

01:14:12.640 --> 01:14:15.720
OK, so what's 116, 104?

01:14:15.720 --> 01:14:17.220
We're going to
increment that count.

01:14:17.220 --> 01:14:18.490
104, 101.

01:14:18.490 --> 01:14:19.570
Increment that count.

01:14:19.570 --> 01:14:21.195
We're going to go
through the sequence,

01:14:21.195 --> 01:14:24.635
and we're going to
count up the bytes.

01:14:24.635 --> 01:14:25.890
OK.

01:14:25.890 --> 01:14:29.070
So now after we
have these counts,

01:14:29.070 --> 01:14:34.380
we're going to find
the pair that occurs

01:14:34.380 --> 01:14:36.570
the most number of times.

01:14:36.570 --> 01:14:38.590
So I guess there's
multiple ones,

01:14:38.590 --> 01:14:42.380
but we're just going to break
ties and say 116 and 104.

01:14:42.380 --> 01:14:45.540
OK, so that occurs twice.

01:14:45.540 --> 01:14:47.530
So now we're going
to merge that pair.

01:14:47.530 --> 01:14:52.200
So we're going to create a
new slot in our vocab, which

01:14:52.200 --> 01:14:54.930
is going to be 256.

01:14:54.930 --> 01:14:57.490
So so far, it's 0 through 255.

01:14:57.490 --> 01:15:00.430
And now we're expanding
the vocab to 256.

01:15:00.430 --> 01:15:04.090
And we're going to say every
time we see 116 and 104,

01:15:04.090 --> 01:15:08.830
we're going to
replace it with 256.

01:15:08.830 --> 01:15:11.155
OK.

01:15:11.155 --> 01:15:15.280
And then we're
going to just apply

01:15:15.280 --> 01:15:18.170
that merge to our training set.

01:15:18.170 --> 01:15:24.770
So after we do that, the
116 and 104 became 256.

01:15:24.770 --> 01:15:28.580
And this 256, remember,
occurred twice.

01:15:28.580 --> 01:15:29.080
OK.

01:15:29.080 --> 01:15:32.860
So now we're just going to loop
through this algorithm one more

01:15:32.860 --> 01:15:33.650
time.

01:15:33.650 --> 01:15:39.850
The second time, it decided
to merge 256 and 101.

01:15:39.850 --> 01:15:44.740
And now I'm going to
replace that in indices.

01:15:44.740 --> 01:15:46.930
And notice that the
indices is going to shrink.

01:15:46.930 --> 01:15:49.000
Right, because our
compression ratio

01:15:49.000 --> 01:15:53.360
is getting better as we make
room for more vocabulary items

01:15:53.360 --> 01:15:56.990
and we have a greater vocabulary
to represent everything.

01:15:56.990 --> 01:16:00.610
OK, so let me do
this one more time.

01:16:00.610 --> 01:16:04.230
And then the next
merge is 257, 3.

01:16:04.230 --> 01:16:07.310
And this is shrinking
one more time.

01:16:07.310 --> 01:16:08.290
OK.

01:16:08.290 --> 01:16:10.570
And then now we're done.

01:16:10.570 --> 01:16:13.920
OK, so let's try
out this tokenizer.

01:16:13.920 --> 01:16:18.890
So we have the string,
the quick brown fox.

01:16:18.890 --> 01:16:23.730
We're going to encode into
a sequence of indices.

01:16:23.730 --> 01:16:27.900
And then we're going to use
our BPE tokenizer to decode.

01:16:27.900 --> 01:16:31.510
Let's actually step through
what that looks like.

01:16:35.540 --> 01:16:38.700
Well, actually maybe decoding
isn't actually interesting.

01:16:38.700 --> 01:16:40.910
Sorry, I should have
gone through the encode.

01:16:40.910 --> 01:16:44.570
Let's go back to encode.

01:16:44.570 --> 01:16:47.190
So encode, you take a string.

01:16:47.190 --> 01:16:48.360
You convert it to indices.

01:16:48.360 --> 01:16:51.950
And you just replay the merges.

01:16:51.950 --> 01:16:53.820
Importantly in the
order that it occurred.

01:16:53.820 --> 01:16:57.245
So I'm going to
replay these merges.

01:17:01.890 --> 01:17:04.576
And then I'm going
to get my indices.

01:17:04.576 --> 01:17:08.836
OK, and then verify
that this works.

01:17:08.836 --> 01:17:11.310
OK, so that was--

01:17:11.310 --> 01:17:12.425
it's pretty simple.

01:17:15.000 --> 01:17:17.800
Because it's simple, it
was also very inefficient.

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:19.960
For example, encode
loops over the merges.

01:17:19.960 --> 01:17:23.160
You should only loop over
the merges that matter.

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:26.100
And there's some other
bells and whistles.

01:17:26.100 --> 01:17:28.600
There's special tokens,
pre-tokenization.

01:17:28.600 --> 01:17:30.840
And so in your
assignment, you're

01:17:30.840 --> 01:17:34.625
going to essentially take
this as a starting point.

01:17:34.625 --> 01:17:37.800
Or I mean I guess you should
implement your own from scratch.

01:17:37.800 --> 01:17:42.000
But your goal is to make
the implementation fast.

01:17:42.000 --> 01:17:44.110
And you can parallelize
it if you want.

01:17:44.110 --> 01:17:46.920
You can go have fun.

01:17:46.920 --> 01:17:49.420
OK, so a summary
of tokenization.

01:17:49.420 --> 01:17:53.220
So tokenizer maps between
strings and sequences

01:17:53.220 --> 01:17:54.990
of integers.

01:17:54.990 --> 01:17:58.780
We looked at character
base by base, word base.

01:17:58.780 --> 01:18:02.300
They're highly suboptimal
for various reasons.

01:18:02.300 --> 01:18:06.220
BPE is a very old
algorithm from '94

01:18:06.220 --> 01:18:08.673
that still proves to be
an effective heuristic.

01:18:08.673 --> 01:18:10.090
And the important
thing is that it

01:18:10.090 --> 01:18:13.510
looks at your corpus statistics
to make sensible decisions

01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:18.250
about how to best adaptively
allocate vocabulary to represent

01:18:18.250 --> 01:18:21.010
sequences of characters.

01:18:21.010 --> 01:18:25.930
And I hope that one day, I
won't have to give this lecture,

01:18:25.930 --> 01:18:27.490
because we'll just
have architectures

01:18:27.490 --> 01:18:30.080
that map from bytes.

01:18:30.080 --> 01:18:34.450
But until then, we'll have
to deal with tokenization.

01:18:34.450 --> 01:18:36.290
OK, so that's it for today.

01:18:36.290 --> 01:18:40.270
Next time, we're going to dive
into the details of PyTorch

01:18:40.270 --> 01:18:42.580
and give you the building
blocks and pay attention

01:18:42.580 --> 01:18:44.600
to resource accounting.

01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:48.080
All of you have presumably
implemented PyTorch programs,

01:18:48.080 --> 01:18:49.930
but we're going to
really look at where

01:18:49.930 --> 01:18:51.260
all the FLOPs are going.

01:18:51.260 --> 01:18:53.970
OK, see you next time.
